## Conventions

<Concrete rules the assistant should follow. Examples:
- Files: kebab-case.ts.
- Each route handler is its own file under `src/api/`.
- All DB queries go through `src/db/queries/`; no inline SQL elsewhere.
- Errors thrown to the user use the typed `ApiError` class; never bare
  `throw new Error`.
- Tests for `src/api/foo.ts` live at `src/api/foo.test.ts`.
- Branch naming: `<author>/<short-slug>`.
- Commit messages: imperative mood, ≤72 chars on subject line.>

## Do

<Bullet list of behaviours you want. Examples:
- Plan changes before writing code for anything touching more than one
  file.
- Cite `path:line` for every reference to existing code.
- Use the existing types in `src/types.ts` rather than redeclaring them.
- Run `<TEST_COMMAND>` before reporting work as done.>

## Don't

<Bullet list of behaviours you don't want. Examples:
- Don't add new top-level dependencies without proposing it first.
- Don't modify `migrations/*` — always create a new migration file.
- Don't write code in any language other than TypeScript.
- Don't add comments that explain WHAT the code does; only WHY when
  non-obvious.>

## Key files and their purpose

<Annotated list of the most important files / directories, so the
assistant can navigate without exhaustively reading the repo. Examples:
- `src/index.ts` — Hono app entry; route registration.
- `src/api/*` — HTTP handlers, one file per resource.
- `src/db/schema.ts` — single source of truth for the DB schema (Drizzle).
- `src/db/queries/*` — query functions; no inline SQL outside this dir.
- `src/lib/auth.ts` — session/JWT helpers; used by middleware.
- `src/lib/errors.ts` — the `ApiError` class and error-to-HTTP mapping.
- `src/types.ts` — shared TypeScript types.
- `docs/architecture.md` — read this before proposing structural changes.>

